response:
 The total area of the region for integer $x$ from $1$ to $n$ is given by the summation $\int_1^n x \left\lfloor \sqrt x \right\rfloor dx$.
To find when $A(n)$ is an integer, we evaluate the integral and become familiar with $\left\lfloor \sqrt x \right\rfloor$. Show some step work. </think>
<answer> Let $\left\lfloor \sqrt x \right\rfloor = i$, which implies $i^2 \le x < (i+1)^2$. The area for integral $x$ from $i^2$ to $(i+1)^2$ is $i^3$.
\begin{eqnarray*}
A(n)&=&\int_1^n x \left\lfloor \sqrt x \right\rfloor dx\\
&=&\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} i^3\\
&=&\frac{(n-1)^2 n^2}{4}
\end{eqnarray*}
The area $A(n)$ is always an integer, hence we seek the number of values for $n$. </answer>

