To show that $S$ is not compact, we can construct a sequence of elements in $S$ that has no convergent subsequence. Let $x_n$ be a sequence in $M$ defined as follows:

$$x_n(k) = \left\{\begin{array}{ll} 1 & \quad \text{if } k = n\\ 0 & \quad \text{if } k \neq n. \end{array} \right.$$

